Chancellor here we are in in a  department store and a lot of people  will be looking and wondering how safe  it is to get out there you're probably  looking at the economy and working out  you know is it going to restart when you  press the button what's your sense of  that particular question well we sent  out a plan some weeks ago for reopening  our country slowly getting our economy  going and we're executing to that plan  and the next stage of that plan is that  we're able to open retail shops like  this from next week and we can do that  because we set out five tests and we're  meeting those five tests thanks  everyone's hard work and I think that's  a good thing so yes we will get going  again and we slowly start to get our  lives back to normal but it won't be the  same as we've been walking around this  department store today you've seen all  the changes the signs the escalators the  screens so people can come back to  stores like this and shop in confidence  knowing that it is safe for them and  their families safe for the staff and I  think that's a very important first step  but these are deliberate these are  measured and these are careful steps and  why does that matter for the economy or  is it matter for unemployment why does  it matter for our livelihoods as we're  coming out of lockdown well I think we  we all want a healthy country we want to  be able to yes go back to work to the  jobs that we love we also want to see  our friends our families get our lives  back to normal and this is knocked down  in many ways has had lots of different  costs it's been necessary but your  people have not been able to go and get  the health care treatment that they need  children have not been at schools people  are suffering from increased mental  health or isolation and yes people as  you pointed out are losing their jobs  and that's going to have an impact not  just on their financial security but on  their long-term health as well so I  think you know it is important now that  we take our steps to rebuild our lives  and that do so in a careful and measured  way and that's what we're here today to  see and if we're not able to open up all  the shops open up all the pubs open up  all the hospitality what are the  consequences well if if you're asking  are there going to be severe  consequences from this knock down and  the answer is  yes and I've been very clear about that  it is gonna have an impact on people's  jobs and their livelihoods that weighs  very heavily and we need to prepare for  that for higher unemployment I think  first of all we should look at what  we've done and that's why I put in place  the furlough scheme which is now  covering almost nine million people  that's protected millions of jobs  through this crisis which is why it was  so important but looking forward you  know that yes there is gonna be hardship  ahead but I want people to know that I  will do absolutely everything I can to  make sure they have the skills the tools  and the support they need to get back  into hopefully an even better job as  quickly as possible you talk about a  plan are you happy with the way that  plan has unfolded with what has happened  in the UK over the course of the past  few months and the government's part in  it yes I'm I'm proud of what we've done  obviously we were faced with an  unprecedented isn't--it's that even  though we have you know one of the worst  death numbers death tolls in the world  you're still proud of your record uh I  would you know chief medical officers  has made this point many times and I  think he's right I think there will be a  time to look on all of this and reflect  and when it comes to numbers I think  that there are ways that he said are  only really possible to compare and  contrast after the fact when we have all  the data in so I think and there will be  a time to that and of course we should  we should do that and be so there's no  where well we should ignore the decision  it's all but I think in order to make  the comparisons that that you're you're  making that we don't have all the data  necessary to make those comparisons  accurately at this point but of course  we should make them no one is hiding  from that fact but if I look at the  measures that we've put in place in  particular around supporting people's  jobs their livelihoods through this  difficult time making sure that their  families have the support they need  making sure businesses you can stay  together  I do think those are significant steps  that have made a real difference to  people's lives and we do have a plan to  reopen are a country reopen our economy  now and we are making progress on that  plan I said that's what we're here today  to talk about so you can see the plan is  working and stores like this will be  open across the country from next week  that shows that we're making  and people should have confidence in  that plan but with the benefit of  hindsight you know could it have gone a  bit better well I've been very clear as  with other ministers been clear when  faced with something of this nature  completely unprecedented both in terms  of the scale and the speed in which it  was happening is it is it the case that  every single thing that we've got  perfectly right first time record of  course that's not going to be the case  and I think most people would look at  that and say you know fair enough but I  mean am I in even in my own economic  interventions I've been as you've known  you know our we've been iterating as we  go that speed and the scale of which  we've had to act has been unmatched  essentially in modern time so that means  that you know I've had to change things  as we go to make sure that the  interventions that we put in place are  getting to the people we want to get  support to and if that wasn't working  first time around and I've made changes  that I that I need to make okay finally  Chancellor obviously you'll have seen  the protests in this country and in the  u.s. about black lives matter  statues being pulled down when you see  that and when you kind of think through  your own experience what's your reaction  as a British Asian of course I know that  racism exists in our country and I know  people are angry they're upset and they  want to see change but what I would also  say is I change doesn't come about  through acts of vandalism or violence  that's not how you achieve real or  lasting change and what I would take a  moment to recognize is you know of  course our story as a country isn't  finished it's a work in progress and it  always will be and there's work to do  but I would take a moment to recognize  the enormous progress we've made as a  country from the time that my  grandparents first arrived here from the  time that I was born and grew up you  know the work that has happened in the  years before now has been tremendous and  everyone deserves credit for that but  that doesn't mean that there's not more  to do so of course there's more to do  and we will work hard at it  have you experienced racism yourself and  do you understand the anger a lot of  people from  absolutely I have I have growing up it's  things in a way that very few other  things have sung me but the things that  might have happened to me many years ago  when I was a kid I think are thankfully  things that would not happen to me today  and that's because of the progress that  our society in our country has made and  I it's something that I'm I'm very proud  of as a country but not not Fanning that  of course there's always work to do as I  said our story is not finished but  there's ways to bring about that change  and I have enormous respect for those  who are doing that in in a reasonable  way that it's not causing violence but  recognizing the issues at stake
 

